#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379889 :question/problem "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $T(2,1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x-{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $T$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,1) & =\\frac{1}{2-{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $T(2,1)$ is the $1$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{1}{x-{y}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ 1 & =x-{y}^{2} \\\\ x & =1+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $T(2,1)$ is the parabola $x=1+{y}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379896 :question/problem "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $M(2,3)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2{y}^{2}-15.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $M$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,3) & =\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}+2(3)^{2}-15=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $M(2,3)$ is the $5$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=5.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2{y}^{2}-15 & =5 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2{y}^{2} & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $M(2,3)$ is the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2{y}^{2}=20.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379903 :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2y}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2y} & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+2y & ={c}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=0$ then the level curve is $y=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2},$ which is not a circle.\n\n- If $c=1$ then the level curve is $y=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2},$ which is not a hyperbola.\n\n- If $c=2$ then the level curve is $y=2-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2},$ which is a parabola.\n\nSo, only statement “$c=2$ gives a parabola” is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379910 :question/problem "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "The level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nNotice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $[0,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider only level curves for $c\\ge 0.$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & ={c}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of ellipses centered at the origin. Therefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/cc/cccaa6d7f554076b3a79364e1f2c4c061ae7cd86f7f0064e3861ba3b035b6a3e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379917 :question/problem "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{y+2{x}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "The level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nNotice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $[0,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider only $c$-level curves for $c\\ge 0.$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\sqrt{y+2{x}^{2}} & =c \\\\ y+2{x}^{2} & ={c}^{2} \\\\ y & ={c}^{2}-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of parabolas with the vertex at $(0,{c}^{2}).$ Therefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/6b/6b425704394d6fcd3090b54a3767b031b728cf4dbc44f9ac9446defe1cdb05aa.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379924 :question/problem "![](images/a3/a3e1daa4db558edc65d65f6bfe25d848de6499fa47695011fa3fb7afda326c2e.png)\n\nWhich of the following could represent the level curves of the surface $z=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $[0,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider only $c$-level curves for $c\\ge 0.$\n\nThe level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =c \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{2c}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in the equations $y=\\pm \\sqrt{2c}$ do not depend on $x.$ So, for a given $c\\ge 0$, the right-hand side of the equation is constant. As a result, we obtain a family of horizontal lines, as shown below.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/9f/9f243d14e996f643e746a2c20c4e606df94420355d7da6211770265af2883619.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379931 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c}\\hspace{0.167em}|=10$ and the angle between vectors $\\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{d}$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{c}$ onto $\\mathbf{d}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{d}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379938 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}\\hspace{0.167em}|=2$ and the angle between vectors $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ is $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{p}$ onto $\\mathbf{q}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380041 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,1,-1)}\\sqrt{x-y+z}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x-y+z}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the expression under the radical is negative. However, the point $(3,1,-1)$ does not cause the  expression under the radical  to be negative, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-3,1,-1)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,1,-1)}\\sqrt{x-y+z} & =\\sqrt{3-1+(-1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3-1-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380048 :question/problem "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1,-2)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z}{{x}^{2}+xy+xz-yz}=$" :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z}{{x}^{2}+xy+xz-yz}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(1,1,-2)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(1,1,-2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1,-2)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z}{{x}^{2}+xy+xz-yz} & =\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+{1}^{2}\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot (-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-2}{1+1-2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380055 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x}=\\frac{{0}^{2}-0\\cdot 2}{{0}^{3}-2\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x(x-y)}{x({x}^{2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}+2} & =\\frac{0-2}{0+2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380062 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+(x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$” is true. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y={x}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{({x}^{2})}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+0} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $1$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380242 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-1)+1}{(-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x(x+y)+(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+1)(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y} & =\\frac{(-1)+1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380650 :question/problem "![](images/51/51ee68a4b95a815cc9372c4d6c1b739464de223375fa301d4cf3cdb1e6e5e829.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{i}$ component) is negative for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y)=\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{K}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the vertical direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $\\mathbf{j}$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{H}(x,y)=-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380657 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $G(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $H(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$  It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $G(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380664 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $M(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It assigns points in  ${R}^{2}$ to vectors in ${R}^{3}$ (not in ${R}^{2}$).\n\n- The function $N(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $P(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It assigns points in  $R$ \n(not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $N(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+1,1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x⟩$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380671 :question/problem "Determine the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{xy}{x-1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $\\mathbf{F}$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4}\\right),\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{xy}{x-1}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4=0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}x\\ne 1\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4,\\hspace{0.167em}x\\ne 1\\}\\hspace{0.167em}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380678 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $C(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x+y\\ne 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $D(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $E(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ to vectors in ${R}^{3}$ (not ${R}^{2}$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $C(x,y)=⟨\\frac{1}{x+y},{x}^{2}{e}^{y}⟩$; $D(x,y)=⟨{y}^{3}-3,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y)⟩$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380685 :question/problem "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y-x}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $\\mathbf{F}$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y-x}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\ny-x>0\\,⟹\\,y>x.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/c7/c78759bc8f9926159065129e00c1fc8b4341ab83ad40cc87e67a2e82b3e6b089.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or equal to zero. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n2-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 2.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ which is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{2},$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/bd/bdf02ff330f256327d0c9f1147c388b25184610b36ea7ab77bb2c570ec3be19a.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/0d/0d3180a60e33c05e904c8fc00fdec4197510198fef3de3fa8c7b3549609205f6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380692 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $Q(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,z\\ne -1,\\,y\\ne 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $R(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $Q(x,y,z)$ $=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i$ $-\\frac{x}{y}\\,j$ $+\\frac{1}{z+1}\\,k$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380699 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $M(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x+y\\ne 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $N(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $P(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $M(x,y,z)$ $=⟨z-{x}^{2},3x+{z}^{2},\\frac{1}{x+y}⟩$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380712 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=\\frac{2y}{1-y}\\,i+\\frac{x}{y-1}\\,j\n$$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,3),(2,-1),$ and $(4,2).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=\\frac{2y}{1-y}\\,i+\\frac{x}{y-1}\\,j\\,:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-2,3) & =\\frac{2(3)}{1-3}\\,i+\\frac{-2}{3-1}\\,j=-3\\,i-j \\\\ F(2,-1) & =\\frac{2(-1)}{1-(-1)}\\,i+\\frac{2}{(-1)-1}\\,j=-i-j \\\\ F(4,2) & =\\frac{2(2)}{1-2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{2-1}\\,j=-4\\,i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/b0/b0b3eced37ace6c64c65cf94c1621d6706894a079621870b19a69b0f27f2c842.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380957 :question/problem "For the function $g(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(y{z}^{2}),$ evaluate $\\frac{∂g}{∂z}$ at the point $(3,\\frac{1}{2},1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $z,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the constant multiple and chain rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(y{z}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(y{z}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{(y{z}^{2})}^{2}}}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(y{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}{z}^{4}}}\\right)\\cdot y\\cdot 2z \\\\  & =-\\frac{2yz\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}{z}^{4}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(3,\\frac{1}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂z}{|}_{(3,\\,1/2,\\,1)} & =-\\frac{2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(1)\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}(1)^{4}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{4}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381244 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ for $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))}{x+y}\\cdot (x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))}{x+y}\\cdot (x\\cdot 0+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))}{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381251 :question/problem "Find ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{3}y).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{3}y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+{x}^{3}y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{x}^{3}y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+{x}^{3}y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{x}^{3}y) & =\\frac{1}{x+{x}^{3}y}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+{x}^{3}y}\\cdot (1+y\\cdot 3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1+3{x}^{2}y}{x+{x}^{3}y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381258 :question/problem "For the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y},\n$$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(\\pi ,-\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}\\right) & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\pi ,-\\pi ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(\\pi ,-\\pi )} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-1)(-1)}{(-1-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381265 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{e}^{y-x}},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the quotient and chain rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{e}^{y-x}}\\right) & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{y-x}-({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y-x})}{{({e}^{y-x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\cdot {e}^{y-x}-({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{y-x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-x)}{{({e}^{y-x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x{e}^{y-x}-({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{y-x}\\cdot (-1)}{{({e}^{y-x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x+{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}){e}^{y-x}}{{({e}^{y-x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{e}^{y-x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,-1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(1,-1)} & =\\frac{2(1)+{1}^{2}-(-1)^{2}}{{e}^{-1-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+1-1}{{e}^{-2}} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381272 :question/problem "Consider the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}.\n$$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{h(x,y)}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381279 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ for $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+{y}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+{y}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & =(\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))+{y}^{5}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =((1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x))+{y}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{y}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =(1+{y}^{5})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381286 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2}),$ evaluate ${f}_{x}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain and the constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})) & =(2x)\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(\\pi /4,1)} & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {1}^{2})+{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot 1+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}+4\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381293 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)={y}^{2}{e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ evaluate ${f}_{y}(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the constant multiple rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}{e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}{e}^{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}{e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{y}+{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{y})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x((2y)\\cdot {e}^{y}+{y}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{y}) \\\\  & =(2y+{y}^{2}){e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(0,2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(0,2)} & =(2\\cdot 2+{2}^{2}){e}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =8{e}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381917 :question/problem "Find ${g}_{y}(x,y,z)$ given that $g(x,y,z)=z\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(xy).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the constant multiple and chain rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =z\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =z\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(xy)^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy) \\\\  & =z\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(xy)^{2}}}\\cdot (x) \\\\  & =\\frac{xz}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382124 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{e}^{xy},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xy})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the chain rule and constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}{y}^{2})+{e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3})+{e}^{xy}\\cdot y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}+{e}^{xy}\\cdot y\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+y{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(-1,1)} & =3(-1)^{2}(1)^{2}+(1){e}^{(-1)(1)} \\\\  & =3+{e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382145 :question/problem "Find ${f}_{y}(x,y,z)$ for $f(x,y,z)=4{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-{e}^{2y}{z}^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-{e}^{2y}{z}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{3}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y}{z}^{4}) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})-{z}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})-{z}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y}) & =4{x}^{3}\\cdot 2y-{z}^{4}\\cdot {e}^{2y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\cdot 2y-{z}^{4}\\cdot {e}^{2y}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}y-2{z}^{4}{e}^{2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}_{y}(x,y,z)=8{x}^{3}y-2{z}^{4}{e}^{2y}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382152 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=x{z}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y),$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(1,1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{z}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{z}^{3})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)) \\\\  & ={z}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={z}^{3}\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\cdot 2x \\\\  & ={z}^{3}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(1,1,2)} & ={2}^{3}-2(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (1)) \\\\  & =8-2(1)(-1) \\\\  & =8+2 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382682 :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}({y}^{2}+1))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}({y}^{2}+1))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}({y}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{2xy}{{y}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{F}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=\\operatorname{ln}({y}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{2xy}{{y}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382689 :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y,z)=3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =6x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-4z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{F}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=6x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-4z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382696 :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy-z)$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}(xy-z))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}(xy-z))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}(xy-z))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{cos}(xy-z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\operatorname{cos}(xy-z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\operatorname{cos}(xy-z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{F}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=y\\operatorname{cos}(xy-z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\operatorname{cos}(xy-z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\operatorname{cos}(xy-z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382703 :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y,z)={e}^{2x-y-z}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2x-y-z})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2x-y-z})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{2x-y-z})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x-y-z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{e}^{2x-y-z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-{e}^{2x-y-z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{F}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=2{e}^{2x-y-z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{e}^{2x-y-z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-{e}^{2x-y-z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382826 :question/problem "![](images/13/13b252e1f9a66836cbd72fbea9bb4c143a71344c923e2215a7718d75c9c42e26.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement $\\text{div}\\,F=0$ is true, whereas statements $\\text{div}\\,F>0$ and $\\text{div}\\,F<0$ are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is solenoidal” is true. Since $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere, the vector field $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{div}\\,F=0$; “$F$ is solenoidal” only."}]}
